\"id\": 130, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 131, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 132, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 133, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 134, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 135, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 136, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 137, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 138, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 139, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 140, \"image_uri\": null}]}, \"error_status\": null, \"metadata\": {}}\n/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_2013_06.txt:| Introduction.—Exchange Stabilization Fund | 98.0 |\n/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_2013_06.txt:| ESF-1.—Balance Sheet | 98.0 |\n/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_2013_06.txt:| ESF-2.—Income and Expense | 99.0 |\n/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_2013_06.txt:| End of fiscal year or month | Total (1) | Airport and Airway Trust Fund (2) | Deposit Insurance Fund (3) | Employees Life Insurance Fund (4) | Exchange Stabilization Fund (5) | Federal Disability Insurance Trust Fund (6) | Federal employees retirement funds (7) | Federal Hospital Insurance Trust Fund (8) | Federal Housing Administration (9) |\n/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_2013_06.txt:International Financial Statistics Capital Movements Foreign Currency Positions Exchange Stabilization Fund\n/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_2013_06.txt:INTRODUCTION: Exchange Stabilization Fund\n/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_2013_06.txt:To stabilize the exchange value of the dollar, the Exchange Stabilization Fund (ESF) was established pursuant to chapter 6, section 10 of the Gold Reserve Act of January 30, 1934 (codified at 31 United States Code 5302), which authorized establishment of a Treasury fund to be operated under the exclusive control of the Secretary, with approval of the President.\n/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_2013_06.txt:Table ESF-1 presents the assets, liabilities, and capital of the fund. The figures are in U.S. dollars or their equivalents based on current exchange rates computed according to the accrual method of accounting. The capital account represents the original capital appropriated to the fund by Congress of $2 billion, minus a subsequent transfer of $1.8 billion to pay for the initial U.S. quota subscription to the International Monetary Fund. Gains and losses are reflected in the cumulative net income (+) or loss (-) account.\n/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_2013_06.txt:Table ESF-2 shows the results of operations by quarter. Figures are in U.S. dollars or their equivalents computed according to the accrual method. \"Profit (+) or loss (-) on foreign exchange\" includes realized profits or losses. \"Adjustment for change in valuation of SDR holdings and allocations\" reflects net gain or loss on revaluation of SDR holdings and allocations for the quarter.\n/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_2013_06.txt:TABLE ESF-1.—Balances as of September 30, 2012, and December 31, 2012\n/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_2013_06.txt:| Net income (+) or loss (-) (see Table ESF-2) | -603398 | -209723 | -813121 |\n/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_2013_06.txt:TABLE ESF-2.—Income and Expense\n/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_2013_06.txt:International Monetary Fund (“Exchange Stabilization Fund,” IFS-1)—(IMF) Established by the United Nations, the IMF promotes international trade, stability of exchange, and monetary cooperation. Members are allowed to draw from the fund.\n/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_2013_06.txt:Special drawing rights (\"Exchange Stabilization Fund,\" IFS-1)—International assets created by IMF that serve to increase international liquidity and provide additional\n/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_2013_06.txt:SDR allocations are the counterpart to SDRs issued by IMF based on members' quotas in IMF. Although shown in Exchange Stabilization Fund (ESF) statements as liabilities, they must be redeemed by ESF only in the event of liquidation of, or U.S. withdrawal from, the SDR department of IMF or cancellation of SDRs.\n/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_2013_06.txt:SDR certificates are issued to the Federal Reserve System against SDRs when SDRs are legalized as money. Proceeds of monetization are deposited into an ESF account at the Federal Reserve Bank of New York.\n/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_2013_06_page_104.txt:INTRODUCTION: Exchange Stabilization Fund\n/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_2013_06_page_104.txt:To stabilize the exchange value of the dollar, the Exchange Stabilization Fund (ESF) was established pursuant to chapter 6, section 10 of the Gold Reserve Act of January 30, 1934 (codified at 31 United States Code 5302), which authorized establishment of a Treasury fund to be operated under the exclusive control of the Secretary, with approval of the President.\n/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_2013_06_page_104.txt:Table ESF-1 presents the assets, liabilities, and capital of the fund. The figures are in U.S. dollars or their equivalents based on current exchange rates computed according to the accrual method of accounting. The capital account represents the original capital appropriated to the fund by Congress of $2 billion, minus a subsequent transfer of $1.8 billion to pay for the initial U.S. quota subscription to the International Monetary Fund. Gains and losses are reflected in the cumulative net income (+) or loss (-) account.\n/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_2013_06_page_104.txt:Table ESF-2 shows the results of operations by quarter. Figures are in U.S. dollars or their equivalents computed according to the accrual method. \"Profit (+) or loss (-) on foreign exchange\" includes realized profits or losses. \"Adjustment for change in valuation of SDR holdings and allocations\" reflects net gain or loss on revaluation of SDR holdings and allocations for the quarter.\n/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_2013_06_page_104.txt:TABLE ESF-1.—Balances as of September 30, 2012, and December 31, 2012\n/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_2013_06_page_104.txt:<table><tr><th>Assets, liabilities, and capital</th><th>Sept. 30, 2012</th><th>Oct. 1, 201